 ## [2.8.0] - 2026-09-03
 
 ### Changed
@@ -28,6 +24,7 @@ and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0
 - `/ai-pr-review dismiss`, `false-positive`, and `wont-fix` on a body-level finding could reply with a raw `::warning::` GitHub Actions annotation glued onto the front of the posted PR comment. The verdict-recording failure path printed it to stdout, the same stream the calling workflow captures as the reply body. It now prints to stderr, matching every other warning in the CLI.
 - Canonical-review selection (`select_canonical`) could pick an empty-body review GitHub auto-creates every time the bot replies to an inline comment: a severity-escalation notice, a recurred-finding reply, or a feedback acknowledgement. Once that empty review became canonical, verdict recording failed with HTTP 422 ("Could not edit a review with a missing body") on every subsequent dismiss/false-positive/wont-fix/fixed, and canonical-review reuse silently stopped updating in place on every rerun. `select_canonical` now skips reviews with an empty or whitespace-only body.
 - A finding's stable `F<n>` ID could be misclassified as body-level, never resolving its thread or dismissing the blocking review, if an older review had once rendered it as an out-of-diff body bullet before it moved to an inline thread in a later run. Classification now prefers the newest review that renders the ID at all, so a finding that has moved buckets between cycles is resolved correctly. A truncated review body (over GitHub's size limit) is also handled: its id-map marker can list an ID whose bullet was cut off by truncation, and that alone is no longer trusted as proof the ID moved inline.
+- **Self-contradictory verdicts marker no longer trusted (issue #755).** A GitHub review's `<!-- ai-pr-review-verdicts: ... -->` marker was observed claiming a finding `"dismissed"` in the very same review body that rendered it as a visible, non-suppressed finding — a state `classify()`'s own logic proves impossible under any normal code path (a truly-dismissed finding is excluded from rendering entirely; a recurring `"fixed"` finding has its verdict overwritten to the `"recurred"` tombstone in the same pass that renders it). The exact write path that produced the contradiction was not found (GitHub Actions job logs had already expired by investigation time, and a live reproduction against realistic prior-review state proved `ai_pr_review`'s own code sends byte-identical, marker-less bodies for both halves of the APPROVE+inline dual-post — see `github.py`'s `_post_new_review`). Trusting a contradictory `"dismissed"`/`"fixed"` entry risks permanently and silently suppressing a finding no human ever acted on, since `classify()` short-circuits to `"suppressed"` on the next run before a human ever sees it again. `merge_verdicts()` now cross-checks each review's verdicts marker against that same review's own id-map and drops any `"dismissed"`/`"fixed"` entry contradicted by a rendered fingerprint in the identical body, logging loudly if it fires. `_apply_classification_side_effects` also gained a narrower, complementary invariant check for the same contradiction, scoped to findings classified `"new"` in the current run.
 
